[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-36488"] :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-8)-9\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37154"] :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-82614/selection/44b37e483a02d7283683d0ab24a8c22c44b33fa86147ee8c5dcd24b251a46ec2" :answer/id #uuid "29046886-e92a-528d-b87d-f656da91227d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82614/selection/ad362d7ed109b6a7fddafec2294f88d02e0a983775d131c6b2ec5ca62befc3b7" :answer/id #uuid "b08e7ce6-ab00-5fce-b07a-accab2990b61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82614/selection/10b1e2f8a97f5e444dd5c20214eb6a23bf68bd785ad41aaafb9dbbacbfd32399" :answer/id #uuid "8192ce68-0b23-5cbe-8693-5c8ee81ff92a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82614/selection/007eeed99df76abca20aac14f6ae83d881dea83014f9bc859cc358ed08ef95fe" :answer/id #uuid "796e8f6a-c08b-5e85-b44d-1815f11ece67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82614/selection/6848bfa3f776003b31df52a22a1e2419b295fd5ff33cfba4f71a666383fa7366" :answer/id #uuid "1c427bec-b70c-5341-87e2-12a7c64dda21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82614/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4523064d-41ad-564c-aee2-06f74cb69951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82614/selection/007eeed99df76abca20aac14f6ae83d881dea83014f9bc859cc358ed08ef95fe" :answer-field/choices ["q-82614/selection/44b37e483a02d7283683d0ab24a8c22c44b33fa86147ee8c5dcd24b251a46ec2" "q-82614/selection/ad362d7ed109b6a7fddafec2294f88d02e0a983775d131c6b2ec5ca62befc3b7" "q-82614/selection/10b1e2f8a97f5e444dd5c20214eb6a23bf68bd785ad41aaafb9dbbacbfd32399" "q-82614/selection/007eeed99df76abca20aac14f6ae83d881dea83014f9bc859cc358ed08ef95fe" "q-82614/selection/6848bfa3f776003b31df52a22a1e2419b295fd5ff33cfba4f71a666383fa7366"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82614" :question/id #uuid "fdb130b3-f386-5a0d-a67b-84c93acff686" :question/math-academy-id "q-82614" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82614/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82614"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82707/selection/47b46be68ad34678fa766cade89f291fe0a1ffd6cd91d28c5fec2147628b960e" :answer/id #uuid "c616a8c6-5f50-5deb-ad69-15fcd020c0c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82707/selection/e53332f78afdd3bab76141116a6a06a54d66b8b1e63e0121bc6b0f952f8fb347" :answer/id #uuid "8a4aeef9-1c15-5088-afdc-995c51ea30c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82707/selection/6a145e4334efe339650b8b70a32aec06578133b46b600eab008353c52a2a70e3" :answer/id #uuid "223b2510-4a91-5872-b337-7b239ede4f96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82707/selection/70ef67354b9df959c4349fe4229a23a972832b7a0a35580cddaaedf3b3e69e67" :answer/id #uuid "9ff572d7-558c-5225-9f64-64e7d1b0971d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82707/selection/ba6503d90d6b572c9cc7470c7b48a7d345a0bf0c91ae0152e2b99638fe248548" :answer/id #uuid "fc3fd2ca-7531-5b90-bca4-818723535a68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82707/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b7f04cf-c5a1-5722-bd71-5614006be7b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82707/selection/70ef67354b9df959c4349fe4229a23a972832b7a0a35580cddaaedf3b3e69e67" :answer-field/choices ["q-82707/selection/47b46be68ad34678fa766cade89f291fe0a1ffd6cd91d28c5fec2147628b960e" "q-82707/selection/e53332f78afdd3bab76141116a6a06a54d66b8b1e63e0121bc6b0f952f8fb347" "q-82707/selection/6a145e4334efe339650b8b70a32aec06578133b46b600eab008353c52a2a70e3" "q-82707/selection/70ef67354b9df959c4349fe4229a23a972832b7a0a35580cddaaedf3b3e69e67" "q-82707/selection/ba6503d90d6b572c9cc7470c7b48a7d345a0bf0c91ae0152e2b99638fe248548"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82707" :question/id #uuid "3a7ee4f4-73c6-5824-a327-bfe7ce4e304c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82707" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 10}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-82707/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82707"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82718/selection/00eaeb95a084cc4966de55205a24a3d176165cd8239e45a7d396e8e56711da85" :answer/id #uuid "b4bd8f5e-192f-5ef0-94df-d81de793e01b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82718/selection/9576b88f964aafa72109b0daf8e5011e1276983566a81ec66c0ae1d06856933c" :answer/id #uuid "1403fe75-03c0-5c2c-9e2b-ad7fdedcce76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82718/selection/2bda3545b14bf989ab1dbee93deb0a17224eeee918f8363b5b98df8bc0cad334" :answer/id #uuid "5835e931-8ee9-5f62-ad12-798f8c51fc95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82718/selection/d6c8cb783886616ed9e7cb469945534df9f0ebb0ebe51be052ae36c0e83d7e64" :answer/id #uuid "4c0521bd-6c6b-5506-8c53-5ce32e76aa51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82718/selection/32470c4dc1fb0f50ca32389ea10138ffbe42f8b57d0edcc5e5110dff4e450c8f" :answer/id #uuid "58878980-24e0-5107-bcdb-7c32a5a68d43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82718/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d311ca08-07f4-5c99-9836-2ef897bc25dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82718/selection/d6c8cb783886616ed9e7cb469945534df9f0ebb0ebe51be052ae36c0e83d7e64" :answer-field/choices ["q-82718/selection/00eaeb95a084cc4966de55205a24a3d176165cd8239e45a7d396e8e56711da85" "q-82718/selection/9576b88f964aafa72109b0daf8e5011e1276983566a81ec66c0ae1d06856933c" "q-82718/selection/2bda3545b14bf989ab1dbee93deb0a17224eeee918f8363b5b98df8bc0cad334" "q-82718/selection/d6c8cb783886616ed9e7cb469945534df9f0ebb0ebe51be052ae36c0e83d7e64" "q-82718/selection/32470c4dc1fb0f50ca32389ea10138ffbe42f8b57d0edcc5e5110dff4e450c8f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82718" :question/id #uuid "69f19ddc-b771-5d32-afa2-60c3aee0640c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82718" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82718/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82718"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
